{
 "description": "Batch place (POST /api/v1/trading/orders/batch), batch cancel (POST /api/v1/trading/orders/cancel-batch) and cancel by client order id (DELETE /api/v1/trading/orders/by-client-id/{client_order_id}). TS placeOrders / cancelOrders / cancelOrderByClientId, Python place_orders / cancel_orders / cancel_order_by_client_id, Go PlaceOrders / CancelOrders / CancelOrderByClientID, Rust place_orders / cancel_orders / cancel_order_by_client_id. The runner uses a fake clock and max retries 3. Each attempt item is one HTTP exchange for the batch call, in order: `network_error` (the request was sent and no answer came: an ambiguous failure), or `http_status` with `data` (a 200's `data` object) or `error` (the error body) and optional `headers`. `lookups` answers GET /trading/orders/by-client-id/{cid} (key `cid:<cid>`) and GET /trading/orders/{id} (key `order:<id>`), with the same item shapes; a lookup is sent at most once and is never retried. Rules: (1) the request body is built once, with every missing client_order_id generated before the first attempt, and every attempt sends the same body bytes (`expect.same_body`); each attempt is signed afresh. (2) An attempt is ambiguous when it ended in a network error after sending, or in HTTP 500, 502 or 504. A 429, 503 or any 4xx is a definite refusal: nothing was acted on. (3) After the final 200, an item's error is reinterpreted ONLY if an earlier attempt of this call was ambiguous: for a place item ALREADY_EXISTS, for a cancel entry INVALID_STATE. Otherwise the item is returned as the server sent it. (4) Place recovery: take order_id and status from the error's details; if either is missing, look the order up by client_order_id; if the lookup fails or answers 404, the item is `unknown`. Never place it again. (5) Cancel recovery: look the order up by order_id (or by client_order_id for a cid entry) and report its status; if the lookup fails, the item is `unknown`. (6) Outcomes: place `placed` (order set, no error), `failed` (error, not reinterpreted), `recovered` (status known), `unknown`; cancel `cancelled`, `failed`, `recovered`, `unknown`. Counts are recomputed from the outcomes, never copied from the server: place `placed` = placed + recovered whose status is not `rejected`; `failed` = failed + recovered `rejected`; cancel `cancelled` = cancelled + recovered whose status is `cancelled`; `failed` = failed + recovered with any other status; `unknown` counts the unknown items. (7) A 429 waits retry_after_seconds and is retried with the same body; details.limit `order_actions` holds only placements (place_order, place_orders) for that wait, not reads or cancels (`expect.hold`). (8) A whole-request 4xx other than 429 is raised at once and never retried; this includes the 400 whose details.limit is a number (the batch is larger than the key's whole budget; the SDK never splits it). `validation` cases are refused locally, before any request; `cid_path` cases give the encoded request path; `cost` gives the client limiter's request tokens and order actions per call. Every `order` object and every 200 lookup `data` is `order_template` overlaid with the fields given (wire names: the order id is `id`). Expected results name it `order_id`: for a placed or recovered-by-lookup item it is the order's `id`, for an item recovered from details it is details.order_id. `orders_count: N` means N limit buys with distinct generated client order ids.",
